Item 06 is done and the gate passes. Six of nine items are now solved.

**What's in `paper2/popularity.md`, under "For the paper":**
- **The three figures.** They are the name-usage bars, the rate heatmap and the citation network. Each has a paragraph on what claim it carries and why the alternative was dropped:
  - the scatter is secondary and mixes two fetch dates;
  - the small multiples take three times the space;
  - the citers-by-decade panels repeat the heatmap.

  Draft captions are included.
- **Print versions.** They are redrawn by a new script, `python -m paper2.section2_figures`, as `paper2/figures/sec2_fig{1,2,3}_*.pdf`. They are vector, with fonts embedded, at most 6.5 in wide, and no text is below 7 pt. The heatmap now uses one hue, so it prints in grey, and the network's labels no longer overlap. I checked each figure by eye after drawing it.
- **The method paragraph.** It covers the OpenAlex queries, the four-field restriction, how the search's word matching pulls in unrelated work, the 700 per-work labels, the pathwidth rule, and the corrected counts.
- **Numbers.** The same script recomputes every number in the captions and paragraph from the cached data, and `tests/test_section2_figures.py` pins them (3 tests).
- **How much depends on the labels.** One model-assisted session made the labels, and no second labeller has checked them; the text says so. As a second reading I used the mechanical phrase rule already in `relevance.py`; it agrees with the labels on 78% of works.
  - **Holds both ways:** pathwidth is more than three times the other eleven names together (3.2× or 3.6×), and the three empty names stay empty.
  - **Does not hold both ways:** MOSP ranks 4th under the labels and 5th under the rule. The paper should say "one of the better-used names", not "fourth".

**Corrections, recorded at the top of PROGRESS.md (none changes a conclusion):**
- The `relevance.py` docstring said the phrase rule was the whole method. The published counts actually come from the labels and the pathwidth rule. I corrected the docstring.
- The pathwidth count of 1,213 includes one OpenAlex record twice, so it is 1,212 distinct works.
- The citation-count column in `popularity.md` is from 2026-09-17 and the network is from 2026-09-29, so four counts differ by one.

The last two are listed for item 07 (the number audit).

**Still open:** the method paragraph cites OpenAlex as "Priem et al. (2022)". I did not fetch that reference this session, so item 08 must check it before it goes into the bibliography.

